IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `theft-nouns`
IMPORT `theft-s34-interpretation`

-- Theft Act 1968, sections 22 to 25: handling stolen goods, advertising rewards,
-- the scope of "stolen goods", retaining a wrongful credit, and going equipped.
-- Layer 1.
--
-- "Dishonestly" in ss 22 and 24A is not the s 2 test: s 1(3) confines ss 2 to 6 to
-- s 1, and the exceptions to s 1(3) in the Act (s 24(4) and (5), s 24A(8), s 25(5),
-- s 32(2)(b), s 34(1)) widen "steal", "theft", "stolen goods" and ss 4(1) and 5(1),
-- not s 2 (NOTES.md, fork 1). It is a fact the person states.
--
-- The Fraud Act 2006 and the Electronic Money Regulations 2011 are named in these
-- sections but are not among the inputs; whether something is "fraud contrary to
-- section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006" or an "electronic money issuer" within the
-- regulation is asked as a fact.

§ `Sections 22 to 25 - stolen goods, wrongful credits, going equipped`

§§ `Section 24 - scope of offences relating to stolen goods`

-- src:150 | (1) The provisions of this Act relating to goods which have been stolen shall apply whether the stealing occurred in England or Wales or elsewhere, and whether it occurred before or after the commencement of this Act, provided that the stealing (if not an offence under this Act) amounted to an offence where and at the time when the goods were stolen; and references to stolen goods shall be construed accordingly.
-- src:151 | (2) For purposes of those provisions references to stolen goods shall include, in addition to the goods originally stolen and parts of them (whether in their original state or not),—
-- src:152 | (a) any other goods which directly or indirectly represent or have at any time represented the stolen goods in the hands of the thief as being the proceeds of any disposal or realisation of the whole or part of the goods stolen or of goods so representing the stolen goods; and
-- src:153 | (b) any other goods which directly or indirectly represent or have at any time represented the stolen goods in the hands of a handler of the stolen goods or any part of them as being the proceeds of any disposal or realisation of the whole or part of the stolen goods handled by him or of goods so representing them.
-- src:154 | (3) But no goods shall be regarded as having continued to be stolen goods after they have been restored to the person from whom they were stolen or to other lawful possession or custody, or after that person and any other person claiming through him have otherwise ceased as regards those goods to have any right to restitution in respect of the theft.
-- src:155 | (4) For purposes of the provisions of this Act relating to goods which have been stolen (including subsections (1) to (3) above) goods obtained in England or Wales or elsewhere either by blackmail or , subject to subsection (5) below, by fraud (within the meaning of the Fraud Act 2006) shall be regarded as stolen; and “steal”, “theft” and “thief” shall be construed accordingly.
-- src:156 | (5) Subsection (1) above applies in relation to goods obtained by fraud as if—
-- src:157 | (a) the reference to the commencement of this Act were a reference to the commencement of the Fraud Act 2006, and
-- src:158 | (b) the reference to an offence under this Act were a reference to an offence under section 1 of that Act.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24 (src:150-158)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24 the goods are stolen goods` g MEANS
        `s 34(2)(b) the thing is goods` g
    AND `s 24(1) the goods come from a stealing, blackmail or fraud that counts` g
    AND NOT (`s 24(3) the goods have ceased to be stolen goods` g)

-- s 24(1), (2) and (4), (5). The proviso in s 24(1): the stealing counts if it was
-- an offence under this Act or, if not, an offence where and when it happened.
-- Goods obtained by fraud count when the fraud is an offence under s 1 of the
-- Fraud Act 2006 (s 24(5)(b)). The original goods, and goods representing them,
-- are tested on the original stealing. Money withdrawn from an account to which a
-- wrongful credit was made counts to the extent it derives from the credit
-- (s 24A(8)).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24(1) (src:150-155)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24(1) the goods come from a stealing, blackmail or fraud that counts` g MEANS
    CONSIDER g's `where the goods came from`
        WHEN `the goods originally stolen, or parts of them` THEN
            `s 24(1) the stealing was an offence under this Act or where and when it happened` g
        WHEN `goods representing stolen goods in the hands of the thief, as proceeds of a disposal or realisation` THEN
            `s 24(1) the stealing was an offence under this Act or where and when it happened` g
        WHEN `goods representing stolen goods in the hands of a handler, as proceeds of a disposal or realisation` THEN
            `s 24(1) the stealing was an offence under this Act or where and when it happened` g
        WHEN `goods obtained by blackmail` THEN
            `s 24(1) the stealing was an offence under this Act or where and when it happened` g
        WHEN `goods obtained by fraud` THEN
            g's `the fraud amounted to an offence under section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006`
        WHEN `money dishonestly withdrawn from an account to which a wrongful credit was made` THEN
            g's `the money, or the part of it asked about, derives from the wrongful credit`
        WHEN `goods of some other origin` THEN FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24(1) (src:150)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24(1) the stealing was an offence under this Act or where and when it happened` g MEANS
        g's `the stealing, or the blackmail, was an offence under this Act`
    OR  g's `the stealing, or the blackmail, amounted to an offence where and when it happened`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24(3) (src:154)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24(3) the goods have ceased to be stolen goods` g MEANS
        g's `they have been restored to the person from whom they were stolen, or to other lawful possession or custody`
    OR  g's `that person and anyone claiming through him have otherwise ceased to have any right to restitution in respect of the theft`

§§ `Section 22 - handling stolen goods`

-- src:145 | (1) A person handles stolen goods if (otherwise than in the course of the stealing) knowing or believing them to be stolen goods he dishonestly receives the goods, or dishonestly undertakes or assists in their retention, removal, disposal or realisation by or for the benefit of another person, or if he arranges to do so.
-- src:146 | (2) A person guilty of handling stolen goods shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding fourteen years.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 22 (src:145-146)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 22(2) penalty for handling stolen goods` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 14)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 22(1) (src:145)
GIVEN h IS A `Handling facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 22(1) guilty of handling stolen goods` h MEANS
        "otherwise than in the course of the stealing" ... NOT (h's `it was done in the course of the stealing`)
    AND "knowing or believing them to be stolen goods" ... h's `he knew or believed the goods were stolen goods`
    AND "stolen goods" ... `s 24 the goods are stolen goods` (h's `the goods`)
    AND "he dishonestly" ... h's `dishonest in dealing with the goods`
    AND "receives ... or undertakes or assists ... or arranges to do so" ...
            (    h's `he received the goods`
             OR  h's `he undertook the retention, removal, disposal or realisation of the goods by or for the benefit of another person`
             OR  h's `he assisted in the retention, removal, disposal or realisation of the goods by or for the benefit of another person`
             OR  h's `he arranged to receive them, or to undertake or assist in their retention, removal, disposal or realisation`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 22(2) (src:146)
GIVEN h IS A `Handling facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 22(2) liable on conviction on indictment for handling stolen goods` h MEANS
    IF `s 22(1) guilty of handling stolen goods` h
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 22(2) penalty for handling stolen goods`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 23 - advertising rewards for return of goods stolen or lost`

-- src:148 | Where any public advertisement of a reward for the return of any goods which have been stolen or lost uses any words to the effect that no questions will be asked, or that the person producing the goods will be safe from apprehension or inquiry, or that any money paid for the purchase of the goods or advanced by way of loan on them will be repaid, the person advertising the reward and any person who prints or publishes the advertisement shall on summary conviction be liable to a fine not exceeding level 3 on the standard scale.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 23 (src:148)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 23 penalty for advertising a reward with no questions asked` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS `the section states no imprisonment`
    `the fine ceiling` IS (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 3)
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 23 (src:148)
GIVEN r IS A `Reward advertisement facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 23 guilty of advertising a reward with no questions asked` r MEANS
        r's `it is a public advertisement of a reward for the return of goods which have been stolen or lost`
    AND (    r's `it uses words to the effect that no questions will be asked`
         OR  r's `it uses words to the effect that the person producing the goods will be safe from apprehension or inquiry`
         OR  r's `it uses words to the effect that money paid for the purchase of the goods or advanced by way of loan on them will be repaid`)
    AND (    r's `he advertised the reward`
         OR  r's `he printed the advertisement`
         OR  r's `he published the advertisement`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 23 (src:148)
GIVEN r IS A `Reward advertisement facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 23 liable on summary conviction for advertising a reward with no questions asked` r MEANS
    IF `s 23 guilty of advertising a reward with no questions asked` r
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 23 penalty for advertising a reward with no questions asked`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 24A - dishonestly retaining a wrongful credit`

-- src:160 | (1) A person is guilty of an offence if—
-- src:161 | (a) a wrongful credit has been made to an account kept by him or in respect of which he has any right or interest;
-- src:162 | (b) he knows or believes that the credit is wrongful; and
-- src:163 | (c) he dishonestly fails to take such steps as are reasonable in the circumstances to secure that the credit is cancelled.
-- src:164 | (2) References to a credit are to a credit of an amount of money.
-- src:165 | (2A) A credit to an account is wrongful to the extent that it derives from—
-- src:166 | (a) theft;
-- src:167 | (b) blackmail;
-- src:168 | (c) fraud (contrary to section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006); or
-- src:169 | (d) stolen goods.
-- src:170 | (3) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:171 | (4) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:172 | (5) In determining whether a credit to an account is wrongful, it is immaterial (in particular) whether the account is overdrawn before or after the credit is made.
-- src:173 | (6) A person guilty of an offence under this section shall be liable on conviction on indictment to imprisonment for a term not exceeding ten years.
-- src:174 | (7) Subsection (8) below applies for purposes of provisions of this Act relating to stolen goods (including subsection (2A) above).
-- src:175 | (8) References to stolen goods include money which is dishonestly withdrawn from an account to which a wrongful credit has been made, but only to the extent that the money derives from the credit.
-- src:176 | (9) “ Account ” means an account kept with—
-- src:177 | (a) a bank;
-- src:178 | (b) a person carrying on a business which falls within subsection (10) below; or
-- src:179 | (c) a person falling within any of paragraphs (a) to (j) of the definition of “electronic money issuer” in regulation 2(1) of the Electronic Money Regulations 2011.
-- src:180 | (10) A business falls within this subsection if—
-- src:181 | (a) in the course of the business money received by way of deposit is lent to others; or
-- src:182 | (b) any other activity of the business is financed, wholly or to any material extent, out of the capital of or the interest on money received by way of deposit.
-- src:183 | (11) References in subsection (10) above to a deposit must be read with—
-- src:184 | (a) section 22 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000;
-- src:185 | (b) any relevant order under that section; and
-- src:186 | (c) Schedule 2 to that Act;
-- src:187 | but any restriction on the meaning of deposit which arises from the identity of the person making it is to be disregarded.
-- src:188 | (12) For the purposes of subsection (10) above—
-- src:189 | (a) all the activities which a person carries on by way of business shall be regarded as a single business carried on by him; and
-- src:190 | (b) “ money ” includes money expressed in a currency other than sterling.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A (src:160-190)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 24A(6) penalty for retaining a wrongful credit` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 10)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

-- s 24A(10), with (12)(a): all the activities a person carries on by way of
-- business are one business. (11) reads "deposit" with the Financial Services and
-- Markets Act 2000, which is not among the inputs; whether money was received by
-- way of deposit is a fact the person states.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A(10) (src:180-182)
GIVEN w IS A `Wrongful credit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24A(10) the business is one within subsection (10)` w MEANS
        w's `the account is kept with a business in the course of which money received by way of deposit is lent to others`
    OR  w's `the account is kept with a business another activity of which is financed wholly or to a material extent out of the capital of or the interest on money received by way of deposit`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A(9) (src:176-179)
GIVEN w IS A `Wrongful credit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24A(9) the account is an account within the section` w MEANS
        w's `the account is kept with a bank`
    OR  `s 24A(10) the business is one within subsection (10)` w
    OR  w's `the account is kept with an electronic money issuer within paragraphs (a) to (j) of the definition in regulation 2(1) of the Electronic Money Regulations 2011`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A(2A) (src:165-169)
GIVEN w IS A `Wrongful credit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24A(2A) the credit is wrongful at least in part` w MEANS
        "(a)" ... w's `the credit derives, at least in part, from theft`
    OR  "(b)" ... w's `the credit derives, at least in part, from blackmail`
    OR  "(c)" ... w's `the credit derives, at least in part, from fraud contrary to section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006`
    OR  "(d)" ... w's `the credit derives, at least in part, from stolen goods`

-- s 24A(5): whether the account is overdrawn before or after the credit is
-- immaterial, and s 24A(12)(b): money includes money in a currency other than
-- sterling. Neither field is read by any rule; theft-cases-s22-25.l4 asserts it.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A(1) (src:160-163)
GIVEN w IS A `Wrongful credit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 24A(1) guilty of dishonestly retaining a wrongful credit` w MEANS
        "(a)" ...
            (    w's `a credit of an amount of money has been made to an account`
             AND w's `the account is kept by him, or he has a right or interest in it`
             AND `s 24A(9) the account is an account within the section` w
             AND `s 24A(2A) the credit is wrongful at least in part` w)
    AND "(b)" ... w's `he knows or believes that the credit is wrongful`
    AND "(c)" ...
            (    w's `he failed to take such steps as are reasonable in the circumstances to secure that the credit is cancelled`
             AND w's `dishonest in failing to take those steps`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 24A(6) (src:173)
GIVEN w IS A `Wrongful credit facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 24A(6) liable on conviction on indictment for retaining a wrongful credit` w MEANS
    IF `s 24A(1) guilty of dishonestly retaining a wrongful credit` w
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 24A(6) penalty for retaining a wrongful credit`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

-- s 24A(3) and (4) are shown as dots in this text and say nothing. Subsections (7)
-- and (8), which add withdrawn money to "stolen goods", are the sixth `Origin of
-- goods` in `s 24(1)` above.

§§ `Section 25 - going equipped for stealing, etc.`

-- src:193 | (1) A person shall be guilty of an offence if, when not at his place of abode, he has with him any article for use in the course of or in connection with any burglary or theft .
-- src:194 | (2) A person guilty of an offence under this section shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding three years.
-- src:195 | (3) Where a person is charged with an offence under this section, proof that he had with him any article made or adapted for use in committing a burglary or theft shall be evidence that he had it with him for such use.
-- src:196 | (4) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:197 | (5) For purposes of this section an offence under section 12(1) of this Act of taking a conveyance shall be treated as theft ... .
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 25 (src:193-197)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 25(2) penalty for going equipped` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 3)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

-- s 25(5). An offence under s 12(1) is treated as theft for this section, so having
-- an article for use in taking a conveyance without authority is the same offence.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 25(1) (src:193)
GIVEN q IS A `Going equipped facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 25(1) guilty of going equipped` q MEANS
        NOT (q's `he was at his place of abode`)
    AND q's `he had an article with him`
    AND (    q's `he had it for use in the course of or in connection with a burglary`
         OR  q's `he had it for use in the course of or in connection with a theft`
         OR  q's `he had it for use in the course of or in connection with taking a conveyance without authority, an offence under section 12(1)`)

-- s 25(3). Proof that he had with him an article made or adapted for use in
-- committing a burglary or theft is EVIDENCE that he had it for such use. It
-- decides nothing: the purpose stays the fact the person states, in `s 25(1)`.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 25(3) (src:195)
GIVEN q IS A `Going equipped facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 25(3) there is evidence that he had the article for such use` q MEANS
        q's `he had an article with him`
    AND q's `the article was made or adapted for use in committing a burglary or theft`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 25(2) (src:194)
GIVEN q IS A `Going equipped facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 25(2) liable on conviction on indictment for going equipped` q MEANS
    IF `s 25(1) guilty of going equipped` q
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 25(2) penalty for going equipped`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

-- s 25(4) is shown as dots in this text and says nothing.
